Date: Wed, 30 Dec 92 11:39:04 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Libel

>I don't mean to belabor the point, Robin, but I can't let this pass. ...
>Who forced anyone to use that Mock Court?  If you're not forced to use it,
>by definition, you cannot be coerced by it.  (The difference between "Mock"
>Court and "Real" Court is that Mock Courts have no power to coerce.)

Mark Pickens and I started out talking about libel, but have drifted
to having a conversation about what it means for something to be
coercive.  We probably both think this subject is less interesting
then how to deal with libel, but as usual feel compelled to get to the
bottom of apparent misunderstandings :-).

Mark originally sought a less coercive alternative to ordinary libel
law, and proposed instead using a Mock Court which only resulted in a
declaration of libel, and not in restitution or (its flip side)
punishment.  *If* one could go to a Mock Court and get it to fairly
judge whether libel had occured or not, without needing the
participation of the person accused of libel, then I would agree this
is a non-coercive solution.

But this seems impractical to me.  Mock Courts often couldn't
reasonably judge the issue without the participation of the accused,
who would have to spend time and effort to defend themself in this
forum.  Thus the accused may have to agree to participate in the
proceedings.  In these cases this approach to libel has the same
status regarding coercion as a libel handled by a court which fined
convicted libelers.  *If* the accused agreed to the proceedings they
are not coercive, otherwise they are.  

Yes the failure of the accused to agree to any court at all might
indicate to others that they were guilty as charged.  But this holds
as well for libel punished by fines instead of public statements.  So
this feature does not distinguish the two cases.  

In summary, when the participation of the accused is required to try
the case, libel punished by statement or by fine are both coersive or
both not, depending on whether or not the accused agree to submit to
this law.  Changing the type of punishment is not a way to get less
coercion, though it may in fact be a good idea.

Robin Hanson  hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov 

