Subject: The moral issues in creating intelligence: references?

Elizabeth Schwartz writes:
>  Another cute SF story is "Valentina: Soul in Sapphire" by Joseph
>Delany and Marc Steigler. An AI created as an Internet Worm wants to
>live, but is being kicked out of computers everywhere. Among other
>things, this AI ends up being incorporated in Texas and testifying in
>court... 
>  I have a small collection of science fiction about AI's and
>programmers. I haven't read any "serious" work that extrapolates far
>into the future of AI. 

I have always been dissapointed that no SF author has, to my
knowledge, written a reasonable "hard" story about AI.  That is, using
a reasonable amout of what we have learned about what real AI would be
like.  Yes, there is still much we don't know, but the little that we
do know is usually sacrificed to the expediency of telling a good
yarn.   

For example, I started an AI in SF reading group at the Lockheed AI
Center when I was there, and we read the Valentina story mentioned
above.  I collect a fairly long list of what we thought were unreal
aspects of the story, and talked to co-author Marc Steigler about
them.  He said they had known about all of them when they wrote the
story, but choose them anyway to make the story tell better.

This is one reason why I much prefer to read non-fiction speculations
about the future.  The Foresight Update had an article by a SF author
on the subject of how SF distorts images of the future a while back.  

