Date: Wed, 30 Dec 92 13:24:02 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: META--MARKET SOLUTION

I think it would be neat if folks here would experiment with some
alternative methods of evaluating and filtering this mailing list, but
I would also like to caution against expecting it to help too much in
the near term.  So please: 1) leave the main list as it is, and 2) let
a thousand flowers bloom in experimental alternative lists/addresses,
which brave souls can try if they wish.  

Email and Netnews have many rather severe problems compared to other
communication media, with quality control being probably the worst.
But the flexibility of the supporting technologies raise hopes that we
may find ways to make this much better than alternaive media.

I worked for many years on "hypertext publishing", and left the field
when I realized that the hard problems really were the social ones in
fitlering and evalutaion, problems which others were ignoring and
which I did not have the resources to address.  I still don't know
what would really work well, but I think I can offer some insights
into the potential problems of several constantly reinvented wheels.

There is a SF book about a planet where ordinary conversation is
filled with people paying each other to talk and listen, with
negotiations prior to each conversation about who pays who how much.
(I forget the title and author, but I borrowed it from
hibbert@xanadu.com)  Clearly something like this is wanted, if
transaction costs can be lowered enough, but having people just pay
per post just to lower traffic, as Sandy Sandfort suggests, may be
worse than having no money involved.  

Having readers evaluate articles, and collecting stats is worth
exploring.  Mimimizing effort required to contribute such evalutations
is crucial.  In general it is also crucial to allow stat consumers to
distinguish types and degrees of evaluators and evaluations, but this
may not be necessary in just this small group.

Taking opinion polls on various proposed claims is also interesting.
It might even be interesting to make bets based on what such polls
would show, though I worry very much that this would reinforce the
image of bets as just another type of opinion poll.  As with all of
these, you of course have to warn against giving any one of these
indications too much weight.

Having people publically evaluate each other is a very touchy area
emotionally; while I agree it should be part of a mature system,
initial attempts must be handled very delicately.  I've done numerical
simulations of games where actors evaluate each other and jocky to be
the most popular.  These simulations aren't encouraging, but folks
have argued that real popularity games work better because they are
much more contrained.  I'd love to see something like the following
actually tried, just to see:

Each person i evalutes each other person j with a weight A_ij, either
directly by stating a number or indirectly through some function of
other people's evalutions.  As a substitute for their own (missing)
opinion on some subject, such as how interesting some post is, they
can then take a weighted average, using this A_ij, of the opinions of
other's on the subject:  

  opinion_i = (Sum_j A_ij opinion_j)/(Sum_j A_ij)

Each person weights each other person's opinion by how much the
respect them.  The complexities of this come when most people don't
have the time to develop considered opinions of each other person, and
so want to defer to other people's opinions of those other people.  A
recursion begins; where does it end?

Perhaps at a fixed point.  If you don't have a considered weight for
the people who have an considered opinion on a subject of interest,
you might use a weighted average of what other people think of them,
weighted by what you think of those other people.  In matrix notation
this is using A*A instead of A (where A has been normalizes so that
Sum_j A_ij = 1).  By continuing this "what others (that I like) think
of others think of ... others think of the article" trend, we might
consider using A*A*A... or A^infinity in place of our own lacking
opinion.  A^infinity has the virtue of definitely using considered
opinions at some level, and is a consensus, since for any i and i',
A^infinity_ij = A^infinity_i'j = w_j, a consensus popularity weight
for each person.

This same consensus comes from the constraint that the consensus
weight for someone should be the weighted average of people's opinion
of them, weighted by those other people's consensus weight.  In matrix
notation A*w = w, or w is an eigenvector of A.

So, people could send their evaluations of each other to an evaluation
server.  Evaluations could be either a particular number, a reference
to the consensus (meaning I defer to everyone else), a particular
weighted average of certain people's opinions, or anything kind of
computation the server wanted to support.  The server which would
publish a consensus weight for each person, weights each person or a
more centralized mailing list could use to filter.  The server would
keep individual evaluations secret, and to avoid these being revealed
by observing the effect of evaluation changes on consensus weights,
there should be a limit on how often consensus is recomputed.  Once a
week or month would probably be enough.  Even with a thousand people,
I think the computational cost to update the consensus weights should
be reasonable at this frequency.  

Unfortunately simulations I have run indicate that to maximize your
consensus weight, the best strategy is to give yourself a very high
weight.  If that is forbidden, join a small mutual-admiration
society.  This is reminiscent of current academic behavior, and is
something I worry about in any popularity based reputation system.
But perhaps other social constraints on what kind of evaluations
people feel they can give each other would reduce this problem.

