I also found the exercises helpful as a reminder.  The first night after
trying them, I had several lucid dreams.  It's been at least ten years
since I've had more than one in a night (that I recall), and at least
several months since having one at all.  I'm so out of practice that I
almost bounced myself out of them by trying to bend the flow of events too
much--so I settled down and let them unfold more "naturally."

Like David, I am puzzled by the fabricated memories in my dreams, but they
do seem to make perfect sense IN the dream.  I wonder if there is any
parallel between this phenomenon and a software program that runs one time
with access to part of a database of rules and facts, and another time
with a different, but overlapping, set of rules and facts.

I truly enjoy the suspension of disbelief in dreamland; there is no point
in doubting that something weird can happen, since it most definitely can,
and probably will, if I bother to think about it.  Consequently, lucid
dreaming helps me to be more willing to notice and accept odd occurrences
while I'm awake.  Is anyone else familiar with Douglas Adam's S.E.P.
(Somebody Else's Problem) invisibility field?  It's a silly fictional plot
device that works on the principle that people often overlook weird things
that don't fit comfortably into their regular world view.  Corny, yes--but,
it rings with truth.  "Weird" (and "truth," for that matter) apparently
applies to different objects and events depending on the current world
state, view, and rules.

It makes me wonder if, after uploading, it would be entertaining or even
useful to run several Me's in parallel with slightly different Rules of
My Universe, then have the resulting Me's discuss the experiences
afterwards to see how I want to continue.  Of course, there is the
significant and much-debated problem of integrating the multiple
personalities.  Maybe I would just punt by adjusting the rule sets,
reverting to a checkpoint backup, and running Me again.  When I was
satisfied with the result, I would keep my rule set analyses for future
reference, then go see what the rest of the observable universe was up
to.

