Date: Wed, 30 Dec 92 21:17:03 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Re: META--MARKET SOLUTION

Robin's suggestion is a fascinating bit of applied mathematics.  But
I think a more useful approach is the idea which he and David Friedman
discussed a few weeks ago: the use of factor analysis to automatically
group people who have common opinions - and even to automatically create
a weighting factor of other people's opinions which will optimally match
my own.  (The analysis also produces a "goodness of fit" measure to
indicate just how well my own tastes regarding a particular message can
be estimated by knowing what persons X, Y and Z think of it.)

If we do begin some experiments with weighting and evaluating posts or
people on this list, I think it would be interesting to try applying
these kinds of analysis to the statistical database that we will collect.
With enough data collected, we could theoretically run mailing list
software which would produce CUSTOMIZED ratings of messages/posters for
each subscriber, based on feedback from others who have been found using
these statistical methods to have similar tastes.

Robin pointed out in the earlier discussion that large databases are
needed for these techniques to really work well, and that accumulating
these databases is the hard part.  But perhaps in our little microcosm
of a world we could get some interesting results.  (Also, the resulting
database could be a valuable asset for ExI in future endeavors.  Oops, I
shouldn't have said that; now everyone will want to get paid to send in
their rankings.:)

Something else interesting might come out of this.  Without going into
much detail here, factor analysis discovers or creates "factors", which
are basically unit vectors in the n-dimensional space which holds the
data.  Each person gets a ranking, a numerical value, in terms of each
factor.  Sometimes, you can look at the rankings, and from what you know
of the people, you can assign physical meanings to the factors.  For
example, in an analysis of various strength tests applied to a large
population, a factor would undoubtedly appear which would turn out to
correspond to sex.

In our database, I wonder whether a factor would appear which we could
identify as extropianism.  Such an identification would inherently be
tentative and controversial, but if we had several people who were
universally agreed to be highly "extropian", and they were distinguished
by high scores on this particular factor, then that would be a plausible
label for it.

If this happened, it could provide a solution to the problem of majority-
rule ratings leading to a takeover of the list (or at least of high-
rated messages) by a non-extropian "silent majority".  This analysis
technique would thus provide a more objective means of keeping the
list ideologically focused.

